          "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "b87adc31-1edd-5006-bb3d-cc48503cf270",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3x+(-2)y}{4}?$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(0,2)$,$(-3,-3)$,$(1,-2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,2)}=\\frac{3(0)+(-2)(2)}{4}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-3)}=\\frac{3(-3)+(-2)(-3)}{4}=- \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-2)}=\\frac{3(1)+(-2)(-2)}{4}=\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/review-3-policy-2-b6fc68f59ef8c5ba/relocation-a/generated-assets/d41901733eec750843883184517119d3303388f97e6d817ee0e8344275762216.png)",
